8
00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:30.724
Ch 09 (run-once jobs), Ch 12 (leases, fencing, clocks), Ch 14 (queues), Ch 40 (SKIP LOCKED workers), What you will build: the platform that runs everything not triggered by a user request.

9
00:00:31.004 --> 00:00:40.988
That includes nightly settlement, reconciliation and GST reports, slot cut-offs, payment-status checks, cart nudges, campaign sends and re-indexing.

10
00:00:41.268 --> 00:00:54.985
You need four pieces: run-once coordination that survives paused processes; firing millions of delayed jobs on time; dependency-aware pipelines; and a shared worker fleet that one team's flood can't take down.

11
00:00:55.265 --> 00:01:00.812
This is the design a distributed cron or task scheduler or Airflow interview question.

12
00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:04.466
The four labs, with their measured results:

13
00:01:04.746 --> 00:01:06.538
L1: run once, really.

14
00:01:06.818 --> 00:01:10.914
Five nodes over 100 ticks, real Redis and PostgreSQL:

15
00:01:11.194 --> 00:01:15.653
No coordination processed 40,400 events more than once.

16
00:01:15.933 --> 00:01:20.605
A Redis lock with plain DEL still duplicated 1,400.

17
00:01:20.885 --> 00:01:23.658
An owner-token lock duplicated 900.

18
00:01:23.938 --> 00:01:30.487
A fencing token claimed on read and checked on write duplicated 0, and rejected 9 stale batches.

19
00:01:31.837 --> 00:01:36.232
A first version that checked the token only on the write still double-processed.

20
00:01:36.512 --> 00:01:39.392
The reason is explained in §2.4.

21
00:01:39.672 --> 00:01:47.544
L2: firing delayed jobs (real PostgreSQL, 120,000 jobs, 30% due in the same second):

22
00:01:47.824 --> 00:01:54.587
Pollers with a 1 s tick reached ~0.5 to 0.9 s p99 lateness.

23
00:01:54.867 --> 00:02:00.925
A 100 ms tick reached 83 ms, at 7,878 queries.

24
00:02:01.205 --> 00:02:07.883
A 2 s lookahead leased into an in-memory timer reached 2 ms p99 with 46 queries.

25
00:02:09.233 --> 00:02:12.113
L3: pipelines as a DAG (simulation).

26
00:02:12.393 --> 00:02:22.419
Cron-chained nightly finance tasks started 1,371 runs on bad inputs and needed a manual re-run on 60 of 60 nights.

27
00:02:22.699 --> 00:02:32.107
A DAG scheduler with automatic retries had 0 bad runs and was correct by 06:00 on 58 of 60 nights.

28
00:02:32.387 --> 00:02:35.033
L4: shared fleet fairness (simulation).

29
00:02:35.313 --> 00:02:41.137
One FIFO queue gave every tenant a 22-minute p99 wait during a marketing dump.

30
00:02:41.417 --> 00:02:46.046
Priority classes broke when a payments retry storm flooded the critical class.

31
00:02:47.396 --> 00:02:55.396
Per-tenant fair queuing, with retries in their own lane, kept first attempts at 0.1 to 1.6 s p99.

32
