34
00:02:59.909 --> 00:03:00.762
Part 1.

35
00:03:01.042 --> 00:03:02.962
Introduction and Why It Matters.

36
00:03:04.212 --> 00:03:06.772
A story: the settlement that ran three times.

37
00:03:07.052 --> 00:03:12.855
The first scheduler was Spring's @Scheduled(cron = 0 0 ) on the settlement service.

38
00:03:13.135 --> 00:03:18.425
It worked until the service scaled to five pods, and then settlement ran five times an hour.

39
00:03:18.705 --> 00:03:20.327
Someone added a Redis lock.

40
00:03:20.607 --> 00:03:22.015
For a month it was fine.

41
00:03:22.295 --> 00:03:22.999
Then:

42
00:03:23.279 --> 00:03:28.441
A pod stalled for 40 seconds (a full GC during a heap-heavy reconciliation).

43
00:03:28.721 --> 00:03:31.388
Its lock expired and another pod took over.

44
00:03:31.668 --> 00:03:34.633
The first pod woke up and posted the same batch again:

45
00:03:34.913 --> 00:03:39.500
38 lakh rupees of duplicate seller payouts, clawed back over two weeks.

46
00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:42.599
Reminder jobs fired late.

47
00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:51.093
Your slot closes in 10 minutes arrived after the slot had closed, because a single poller scanned a 40-million-row table once a minute.

48
00:03:51.373 --> 00:03:54.061
The nightly pipeline was a chain of cron times.

49
00:03:54.341 --> 00:04:01.231
GST reports started at 02:30 because settlement is usually done by 02:15.

50
00:04:01.511 --> 00:04:04.882
On month-end nights it wasn't, and the reports went out wrong.

51
00:04:05.162 --> 00:04:11.178
Marketing enqueued 5 million notification jobs into the same queue as payment-status checks.

52
00:04:11.458 --> 00:04:16.493
Payments sat pending for 40 minutes, and customers were charged twice when they retried.

53
00:04:17.843 --> 00:04:20.701
A scheduler looks like a while (true) sleep; run();.

54
00:04:20.981 --> 00:04:35.744
In practice it is a distributed-systems problem (leases, fencing, clocks), a database problem (queue tables at high churn), a workflow problem (dependencies, retries, backfills), and a multi-tenant capacity problem.

55
00:04:37.094 --> 00:04:37.777
Quote:

56
00:04:38.057 --> 00:04:39.721
The thesis of this chapter.

57
00:04:40.001 --> 00:04:43.201
Run exactly once is a lease plus a check at the resource.

58
00:04:43.481 --> 00:04:54.339
Locks expire under paused processes, so the store must reject stale holders using a fencing token that is checked on read and write, or the work must be idempotent (L1).

59
00:04:54.619 --> 00:05:03.003
Delayed jobs are fired from a durable table plus an in-memory timer with short lookahead leases, not from tight polling (L2).

60
00:05:03.283 --> 00:05:12.713
Pipelines declare dependencies; the scheduler starts work when inputs are ready, retries automatically, and prioritises the critical path (L3).

61
00:05:12.993 --> 00:05:22.571
A shared fleet isolates tenants, not priorities: fair queuing, retry lanes and weights keep one team's flood from becoming everyone's outage (L4).

