132
00:07:26.486 --> 00:07:27.361
Part 2.

133
00:07:27.641 --> 00:07:29.518
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

134
00:07:30.768 --> 00:07:33.285
Intuition: a hospital's duty roster.

135
00:07:33.565 --> 00:07:35.549
Think of a hospital's duty roster:

136
00:07:35.829 --> 00:07:38.453
One nurse holds the medication keys per shift.

137
00:07:38.733 --> 00:07:47.928
If she's delayed, the next shift can't take the keys and have her still dispensing: the keys have a numbered tag, and the pharmacy only honours the newest tag.

138
00:07:48.208 --> 00:07:51.877
Appointment reminders go out from a card index sorted by time.

139
00:07:52.157 --> 00:07:56.531
Someone pulls the next hour's cards each hour and calls exactly on time.

140
00:07:56.811 --> 00:08:02.101
Surgery, recovery and discharge happen when the previous step is done, not at fixed hours.

141
00:08:03.451 --> 00:08:10.577
When one ward floods the lab with samples, the lab keeps separate queues so the emergency ward's samples aren't stuck behind them.

142
00:08:10.857 --> 00:08:18.643
That is a scheduler: leases with tokens, a time-sorted index plus a timer, dependency-driven workflows, and fair queues.

143
00:08:19.993 --> 00:08:20.953
Requirements.

144
00:08:21.233 --> 00:08:22.087
Functional.

145
00:08:22.367 --> 00:08:29.556
Recurring jobs (cron expressions with time zones), one-off delayed jobs (run at T), and workflows (DAGs).

146
00:08:29.836 --> 00:08:35.447
At-least-once execution with idempotency support, or exactly-once effects via fencing.

147
00:08:35.727 --> 00:08:40.591
Retries with backoff, timeouts, dead-letter handling; manual re-run and backfill.

148
00:08:40.871 --> 00:08:46.353
Tenants with quotas, priorities and isolation; pause or resume per job or tenant.

149
00:08:47.703 --> 00:08:50.797
History: every run, its inputs, outcome and logs.

150
00:08:51.077 --> 00:08:52.186
Non-functional.

151
00:08:52.466 --> 00:09:00.167
Delayed jobs fire within 1 s of due (p99) at millions per day and bursts of tens of thousands per second.

152
00:09:00.447 --> 00:09:05.866
No lost jobs across node failures; no double effects from paused or partitioned nodes.

153
00:09:06.146 --> 00:09:12.397
Scheduler availability 99.95%, since many business processes depend on it.

