00:14:41.433 --> 00:14:42.841
The tick bounds lateness.

274
00:14:43.121 --> 00:14:46.854
Work that falls due while pollers sleep waits up to a whole interval.

275
00:14:47.134 --> 00:15:00.830
Poller p99s varied between runs (W1 from 175 to 527 ms across our runs; W2 from 337 to 892 ms).

276
00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:03.243
The max column is the stable bound.

277
00:15:03.523 --> 00:15:09.753
W1's full-scan claims were so slow that the queue was rarely empty, so it hardly ever slept.

278
00:15:10.033 --> 00:15:14.363
It was effectively continuous polling, paid for with a table scan per query.

279
00:15:15.713 --> 00:15:16.972
Queue tables churn.

280
00:15:17.252 --> 00:15:23.972
Every claim is an UPDATE and a commit, and the head of a dueat index fills with dead entries for jobs just claimed.

281
00:15:24.252 --> 00:15:39.527
We verified this with EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS): a clean index read 8 buffers in 0.2 ms, and after 60k claims it read 718 buffers in 10.5 ms, until index hint bits cleaned it up.

282
00:15:39.807 --> 00:15:50.580
Remedies: aggressive autovacuum on the table, partitioning by due time (drop old partitions instead of deleting rows), or moving done rows to a history table.

283
00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:52.204
Lookahead + timer:

284
00:15:53.554 --> 00:16:00.786
Lease the next few seconds of jobs in large batches (status = 'L', leaseuntil = now() + 30 s).

285
00:16:01.066 --> 00:16:05.930
Hold them in a DelayQueue or hashed timing wheel, and fire each at its due instant.

286
00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:07.917
Write results back in batches.

287
00:16:08.197 --> 00:16:12.421
If the node dies, the leases expire and another node picks the jobs up.

288
00:16:12.701 --> 00:16:15.922
This is at-least-once, so the jobs must be idempotent.

289
00:16:17.272 --> 00:16:26.040
Sharding: partition jobs by hash(id) % N or by tenant, with one lookahead loader per partition (lease the partition itself).

290
00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:33.808
Hierarchical timing wheels (Kafka's purgatory, Netty's HashedWheelTimer) make in-memory timers O(1) per job.

291
00:16:34.088 --> 00:16:39.912
Round-time bursts (everything due at 18:00:00) are the real test.

292
00:16:40.192 --> 00:16:47.168
They need batch claims, parallel fire workers, and, where the business allows, jittering due times by a few seconds.

293
00:16:48.518 --> 00:16:50.843
Recurring jobs, misfires and time.

294
00:16:51.123 --> 00:16:53.406
Cron in a time zone: store the zone.

295
00:16:53.686 --> 00:17:01.771
Daylight-saving transitions (not in India, but in other markets) create skipped and repeated local times; define the behaviour.

296
00:17:02.051 --> 00:17:05.934
Materialise the next run as a delayed job (nextfireat).

297
00:17:06.214 --> 00:17:08.369
When it fires, compute the following one.

298
00:17:08.649 --> 00:17:12.681
This way recurring jobs reuse §2.5's machinery.

299
00:17:12.961 --> 00:17:24.630
Misfire policy after downtime: fire once now (coalesce), fire all missed (catch-up, for example, hourly settlements must all happen), or skip to the next (a dashboard refresh).

300
00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:26.041
Choose per job.

301
00:17:27.391 --> 00:17:31.081
Clock skew: fire based on the scheduler's clock, not the workers'.

302
00:17:31.361 --> 00:17:37.292
Use leases with generous margins, and never compare timestamps from different machines for correctness.

