707
00:45:08.678 --> 00:45:10.918
500 runs for 100 ticks).

708
00:45:12.268 --> 00:45:13.185
Watch out:

709
00:45:13.465 --> 00:45:14.169
2.

710
00:45:14.449 --> 00:45:16.689
Locks treated as mutual exclusion.

711
00:45:16.969 --> 00:45:21.087
A lock with a TTL is a lease; paused holders don't know it expired.

712
00:45:22.437 --> 00:45:23.354
Watch out:

713
00:45:23.634 --> 00:45:24.381
3.

714
00:45:24.661 --> 00:45:26.303
Releasing with plain DEL.

715
00:45:26.583 --> 00:45:28.397
It deletes the next holder's lock.

716
00:45:29.747 --> 00:45:30.664
Watch out:

717
00:45:30.944 --> 00:45:31.648
4.

718
00:45:31.928 --> 00:45:33.464
Fencing only the write.

719
00:45:33.744 --> 00:45:37.989
A new holder can read before the stale write lands (L1's first version).

720
00:45:39.339 --> 00:45:40.257
Watch out:

721
00:45:40.537 --> 00:45:41.305
5.

722
00:45:41.585 --> 00:45:43.739
Polling a big table without an index.

723
00:45:44.019 --> 00:45:45.982
A full scan per poll (L2).

724
00:45:47.332 --> 00:45:48.249
Watch out:

725
00:45:48.529 --> 00:45:49.276
6.

726
00:45:49.556 --> 00:45:51.967
Treating a queue table like any other table.

727
00:45:52.247 --> 00:45:54.849
Dead tuples at the head, bloat, slow claims.

728
00:45:56.199 --> 00:45:57.117
Watch out:

729
00:45:57.397 --> 00:45:58.143
7.

730
00:45:58.423 --> 00:46:00.429
Everything due at:00.

731
00:46:00.709 --> 00:46:02.010
Round-time bursts.

732
00:46:02.290 --> 00:46:03.399
Jitter when allowed.

733
00:46:04.749 --> 00:46:05.667
Watch out:

734
00:46:05.947 --> 00:46:06.629
8.

735
00:46:06.909 --> 00:46:08.851
Chaining pipelines by clock time.

736
00:46:09.131 --> 00:46:11.499
Wrong outputs on slow nights (L3).

737
00:46:12.849 --> 00:46:13.766
Watch out:

738
00:46:14.046 --> 00:46:14.793
9.

739
00:46:15.073 --> 00:46:16.545
Non-idempotent jobs.

740
00:46:16.825 --> 00:46:19.363
At-least-once execution will duplicate them.

741
00:46:20.713 --> 00:46:21.631
Watch out:

742
00:46:21.911 --> 00:46:22.615
10.

743
00:46:22.895 --> 00:46:24.836
One FIFO queue for all tenants.

744
00:46:25.116 --> 00:46:27.121
One flood blocks everyone (L4).

745
00:46:28.471 --> 00:46:29.389
Watch out:

746
00:46:29.669 --> 00:46:30.501
11.

747
00:46:30.781 --> 00:46:33.277
Retries in the same lane as first attempts.

748
00:46:33.557 --> 00:46:36.543
A tenant's own storm blocks its fresh work (L4).

749
00:46:37.893 --> 00:46:38.811
Watch out:

750
00:46:39.091 --> 00:46:39.880
12.

751
00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:41.632
No misfire policy.

752
00:46:41.912 --> 00:46:46.029
After an outage, either a flood of catch-up runs or silently missed ones.

753
00:46:47.379 --> 00:46:48.254
Part 8.

754
00:46:48.534 --> 00:46:49.878
Interview Q and A.

755
00:46:51.128 --> 00:46:51.960
Beginner.

756
00:46:52.240 --> 00:46:53.264
Question 1.

757
00:46:53.544 --> 00:46:57.384
How do you make a scheduled job run on only one of many instances?

758
00:46:57.664 --> 00:46:59.029
Thirty-second answer:

759
00:46:59.309 --> 00:47:11.533
Acquire a lease (a lock with a TTL) before running: a DB row lock or lease, etcd or ZooKeeper, or Redis SET NX PX with an owner token and compare-and-delete release.

760
00:47:11.813 --> 00:47:20.005
Because leases can expire under paused processes, also fence the effect (a token the resource checks) or make the job idempotent.

761
00:47:21.355 --> 00:47:22.358
Question 2.

762
00:47:22.638 --> 00:47:24.942
How would you store millions of delayed jobs?

763
00:47:25.222 --> 00:47:26.587
Thirty-second answer:

764
00:47:26.867 --> 00:47:29.790
A durable table (or log) keyed by due time:

765
00:47:30.070 --> 00:47:46.731
(id, dueat, status, leaseuntil, attempts, payload, tenant), indexed or partitioned by due time; loaders lease upcoming jobs in batches into in-memory timers; workers run them and write results back in batches; leases expire if nodes die.

766
00:47:48.081 --> 00:47:49.127
Question 3.

767
00:47:49.407 --> 00:47:52.180
What execution guarantee should a scheduler offer?

768
00:47:52.460 --> 00:47:53.825
Thirty-second answer:

769
00:47:54.105 --> 00:48:03.833
At-least-once, with idempotency keys per logical run; exactly-once effects come from idempotent handlers or fencing, not from the scheduler alone.

770
00:48:05.183 --> 00:48:06.250
Question 4.

771
00:48:06.530 --> 00:48:08.535
How do retries work in a scheduler?

772
00:48:08.815 --> 00:48:10.181
Thirty-second answer:

773
00:48:10.461 --> 00:48:22.813
Classify errors as retryable versus permanent; retry with exponential backoff and jitter up to a max; then dead-letter with alerting; keep retries in their own lane so they don't block fresh work.

774
00:48:24.163 --> 00:48:25.165
Intermediate.

775
00:48:25.445 --> 00:48:26.555
Question 5.

776
00:48:26.835 --> 00:48:29.480
What is a fencing token and why is it needed?

777
00:48:29.760 --> 00:48:31.125
Thirty-second answer:

778
00:48:31.405 --> 00:48:40.984
A monotonically increasing number issued with each lease; the protected resource records the highest token it has seen and rejects operations with older ones.

779
00:48:41.264 --> 00:48:46.128
Needed because a lease holder can pause past expiry and act on stale authority.

780
00:48:47.478 --> 00:48:48.587
Question 6.

781
00:48:48.867 --> 00:48:52.238
How do you handle recurring jobs after scheduler downtime?

782
00:48:52.518 --> 00:48:53.883
Thirty-second answer:

783
00:48:54.163 --> 00:49:04.041
A per-job misfire policy: fire once (coalesce), fire every missed run (catch-up, for things like hourly settlements), or skip to the next (refreshes).

784
00:49:04.321 --> 00:49:08.289
Materialise the next fire time so the scheduler knows what was missed.

785
00:49:09.639 --> 00:49:10.727
Question 7.

786
00:49:11.007 --> 00:49:14.036
Why are queue tables in a relational database tricky?

787
00:49:14.316 --> 00:49:15.681
Thirty-second answer:

788
00:49:15.961 --> 00:49:28.015
High churn: every job is inserted, claimed (UPDATE), completed and deleted; dead tuples accumulate, especially at the head of the due-time index where claims happen; claims contend.

789
00:49:28.295 --> 00:49:35.036
Use SKIP LOCKED, batching, partitioning by time (drop old partitions), history tables, and vacuum tuning.

790
00:49:36.386 --> 00:49:37.410
Question 8.

791
00:49:37.690 --> 00:49:39.909
How would you design cron for many teams?

792
00:49:40.189 --> 00:49:41.554
Thirty-second answer:

793
00:49:41.834 --> 00:49:58.047
Definitions with cron expression + time zone + misfire policy + owner; a planner materialises next runs as delayed jobs; firing goes through the same delayed-job path; tenant quotas and lanes; run history and alerting per owner.

794
00:49:59.397 --> 00:50:00.464
Question 9.

795
00:50:00.744 --> 00:50:06.504
How do you fire 50,000 jobs due at exactly 18:00:00?

796
00:50:06.784 --> 00:50:08.149
Thirty-second answer:

797
00:50:08.429 --> 00:50:21.741
Lease them ahead (lookahead), fire from in-memory timers across parallel workers, batch write-backs, and where allowed, jitter due times by a few seconds; ensure downstream capacity or rate limits for the burst.

798
00:50:23.091 --> 00:50:23.859
Senior.

799
00:50:24.139 --> 00:50:25.185
Question 10.

800
00:50:25.465 --> 00:50:29.113
Cron chaining versus a DAG engine for batch pipelines?

801
00:50:29.393 --> 00:50:30.758
Thirty-second answer:

802
00:50:31.038 --> 00:50:38.270
Cron chaining encodes duration guesses; slow nights and failures make downstream tasks run on missing inputs.

803
00:50:38.550 --> 00:50:48.107
A DAG engine starts tasks when inputs are ready, retries automatically, records runs, supports backfills, and can prioritise the critical path.

804
00:50:49.457 --> 00:50:50.588
Question 11.

805
00:50:50.868 --> 00:50:53.300
How do you keep one tenant from starving others?

806
00:50:53.580 --> 00:50:54.945
Thirty-second answer:

807
00:50:55.225 --> 00:51:06.788
Per-tenant queues with weighted fair queuing (DRR), per-tenant concurrency caps and quotas, admission control at enqueue, separate retry lanes, and physical pools for the most critical work.

808
00:51:08.138 --> 00:51:09.226
Question 12.

809
00:51:09.506 --> 00:51:11.447
How do you scale the scheduler itself?

810
00:51:11.727 --> 00:51:13.093
Thirty-second answer:

811
00:51:13.373 --> 00:51:29.629
Shard jobs by hash or tenant; each shard is owned by one loader via a lease; loaders scale horizontally; workers scale per lane; the DB is partitioned by time; the DAG engine's metadata DB is separate from job execution.

812
00:51:30.979 --> 00:51:32.259
Question 13.

813
00:51:32.539 --> 00:51:34.651
How do you make long-running jobs safe?

814
00:51:34.931 --> 00:51:36.296
Thirty-second answer:

815
00:51:36.576 --> 00:51:49.333
Heartbeats extend leases; checkpoints let restarts resume; each step checks its fencing token; cancellation is cooperative; timeouts are set per job type; effects are idempotent per step.

816
00:51:50.683 --> 00:51:51.878
Question 14.

817
00:51:52.158 --> 00:51:53.950
What would you monitor on a scheduler?

818
00:51:54.230 --> 00:51:55.595
Thirty-second answer:

819
00:51:55.875 --> 00:52:10.446
Lateness per tenant and job type; backlog by due time; leases and fenced writes; retries and DLQ; DAG SLA projections; per-tenant waits and quota use; worker utilisation; run failures by owner.

820
00:52:11.796 --> 00:52:12.692
Architect.

821
00:52:12.972 --> 00:52:14.167
Question 15.

822
00:52:14.447 --> 00:52:17.433
Design a distributed job scheduler end to end.

823
00:52:17.713 --> 00:52:19.079
Thirty-second answer:

824
00:52:19.359 --> 00:52:39.625
API and definitions; delayed-job store partitioned by due time; sharded lookahead loaders with leases and timing wheels; fair per-tenant dispatch with retry lanes; idempotent or fenced handlers with heartbeats; DAG engine for workflows; misfire policies; run history; observability and quotas.

825
00:52:40.975 --> 00:52:42.170
Question 16.

826
00:52:42.450 --> 00:52:43.666
Build versus buy:

827
00:52:43.946 --> 00:52:46.549
Quartz, Airflow, Temporal, cloud schedulers?

828
00:52:46.829 --> 00:52:48.194
Thirty-second answer:

829
00:52:48.474 --> 00:53:05.925
Quartz (clustered, JDBC store) suits in-app recurring jobs; Airflow or Dagster suit batch DAGs; Temporal or Step Functions suit durable workflows and sagas; cloud schedulers (EventBridge Scheduler, Cloud Tasks) suit delayed triggers at scale.

830
00:53:06.205 --> 00:53:09.490
Build only the multi-tenant glue and policies you need.

831
00:53:10.840 --> 00:53:12.184
Question 17.

832
00:53:12.464 --> 00:53:14.597
How do you test a scheduler's correctness?

833
00:53:14.877 --> 00:53:16.243
Thirty-second answer:

834
00:53:16.523 --> 00:53:28.832
Chaos tests: pause processes past lease TTLs (SIGSTOP), kill nodes, partition networks, skew clocks; assert invariants (no duplicate effects, no lost jobs, lateness bounds).

835
00:53:29.112 --> 00:53:34.147
Deterministic simulation for timing logic; load tests with round-time bursts.

836
00:53:35.497 --> 00:53:36.670
Question 18.

837
00:53:36.950 --> 00:53:40.001
How do time zones and DST affect scheduling?

838
00:53:40.281 --> 00:53:41.646
Thirty-second answer:

839
00:53:41.926 --> 00:53:57.051
Store schedules in the owner's zone; compute fire times in UTC; on DST gaps skip or shift, on overlaps run once or twice by policy; business days and holidays from calendars; never rely on server local time.

840
00:53:58.401 --> 00:53:59.639
Question 19.

841
00:53:59.919 --> 00:54:02.820
How does the scheduler interact with sagas and events?

842
00:54:03.100 --> 00:54:04.465
Thirty-second answer:

843
00:54:04.745 --> 00:54:17.780
Timers drive saga timeouts (payment pending 15 min, then expire), reservation expiries and follow-ups; jobs publish events via outbox; durable execution engines combine both.

844
00:54:18.060 --> 00:54:21.815
The scheduler must be as reliable as the flows that depend on it.

845
00:54:23.165 --> 00:54:24.274
Question 20.

846
00:54:24.554 --> 00:54:27.071
What does 'healthy' look like for the job platform?

847
00:54:27.351 --> 00:54:28.717
Thirty-second answer:

848
00:54:28.997 --> 00:54:42.927
Lateness within SLO per tenant; zero duplicate effects; DAG SLAs met with early warnings; per-tenant waits within budgets during floods; DLQ small and owned; run history complete; leases stable.

849
00:54:44.277 --> 00:54:45.173
Part 9.

850
00:54:45.453 --> 00:54:46.861
Summary and Practice.

851
00:54:48.111 --> 00:54:49.199
Key takeaways.

852
00:54:49.479 --> 00:54:50.695
Locks are leases.

853
00:54:50.975 --> 00:54:55.370
Fence at the resource on read and write, or make effects idempotent (L1).

854
00:54:55.650 --> 00:55:02.647
Delayed jobs: a durable table partitioned by time, plus lookahead leases into in-memory timers (L2).

855
00:55:02.927 --> 00:55:08.645
Pipelines are DAGs with retries and critical-path priority, not clock chains (L3).

856
00:55:08.925 --> 00:55:13.789
Isolate tenants, not priorities: fair queues, retry lanes, quotas (L4).

857
00:55:14.069 --> 00:55:19.530
Idempotency, misfire policies and time zones are part of every job definition.

858
00:55:20.880 --> 00:55:22.203
Decision cheat sheet.

859
00:55:22.483 --> 00:55:23.827
Here is a quick table:

860
00:55:24.107 --> 00:55:25.280
Need, and Choice.

861
00:55:25.560 --> 00:55:26.712
Singleton job:

862
00:55:26.992 --> 00:55:30.811
Lease + fencing token (etcd revision) or idempotent effects.

863
00:55:31.091 --> 00:55:32.179
Delayed jobs:

864
00:55:32.459 --> 00:55:35.339
Partitioned table + lookahead + timing wheel.

865
00:55:35.619 --> 00:55:36.493
Recurring:

866
00:55:36.773 --> 00:55:40.485
Materialised next run + misfire policy + zone.

867
00:55:40.765 --> 00:55:41.704
Workflows:

868
00:55:41.984 --> 00:55:45.269
DAG engine; durable execution for sagas.

869
00:55:45.549 --> 00:55:46.637
Multi-tenant:

870
00:55:46.917 --> 00:55:49.840
Weighted fair queues, retry lanes, quotas, caps.

871
00:55:50.120 --> 00:55:50.931
Bursts:

872
00:55:51.211 --> 00:55:53.472
Batch claims, parallel fire, jitter.

873
